"A Counterexample to a Question of Hof, Knill and Simon (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v21i3p11/pdf/)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.3 years ago; Sébastien Labbé* ... In this article, we give a negative answer to a question of Hof, Knill and Simon (1995) concerning purely morphic sequences obtained from primitive morphism containing an infinite number of palindromes. Their conjecture states that such palindromic sequences arise from substitutions that are in class *P*. ... For example, on the binary alphabet A = {a, b}, the morphism ρ : a ↦ bbaba, b ↦ bba is in class *P*.\nA Counterexample to a Question of Hof, Knill and Simon\n\nSébastien Labbé*\n\nLIAFA, Université Paris Diderot - Paris 7,\nCase 7014, 75205 Paris Cedex 13, France\nlabbe@liafa.univ-paris-diderot.fr\n\nSubmitted: Sep 27, 2013; Accepted: Jul 9, 2014; Published: Jul 21, 2014\nMathematics Subject Classifications: 68R15, 37B10\n\n**Abstract**\n\nIn this article, we give a negative answer to a question of Hof, Knill and Simon (1995) concerning purely morphic sequences obtained from primitive morphism containing an infinite number of palindromes. Their conjecture states that such palindromic sequences arise from substitutions that are in class *P*. The conjecture was proven for the binary alphabet by B. Tan in 2007. We give here a counterexample for a ternary alphabet.\n\n**Keywords:** Hof, Knill and Simon conjecture; class *P*; palindromes; stabilizer.\n\n# 1 Introduction\n\nA morphism of monoid φ : A* → A* is in class *P* if there exists a palindrome p and for every α ∈ A there exists a palindrome qα such that φ(α) = pqα [HKS95]. If x is an infinite sequence generated by a morphism in class *P*, then Hof, Knill and Simon proved that x contains an infinite number of palindrome factors. Such morphisms allowed them to derive some discrete Schrödinger operators having purely singular continuous spectrum. For example, on the binary alphabet A = {a, b}, the morphism ρ : a ↦ bbaba, b ↦ bba is in class *P*. The morphism θ : a ↦ ab, b ↦ ba is not in class *P*, but its square θ² : a ↦ abba, b ↦ baab is in class *P*. Therefore, the fixed point\n\nxρ = ρ(xρ) = bbabbabbababbabbababbabbababbabbababbabbababbabbab ···\n\nand the fixed point, also known as the Thue-Morse word,\n\nxθ = θ(xθ) = abbabaabbaababbaababbaababbaababbaababbaababba ···\n\n*With the support of NSERC (Canada)\n\nTHE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS 21(3) (2014), #P3.11\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Counterexample to a Question of Hof, Knill and Simon | The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v21i3p11)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # A Counterexample to a Question of Hof, Knill and Simon\n\n  * Sébastien Labbé ... Keywords: Hof, Knill and Simon conjecture, class P, palindromes, stabilizer. ... In this article, we give a negative answer to a question of Hof, Knill and Simon (1995) concerning purely morphic sequences obtained from primitive morphism containing an infinite number of palindromes.\n\n# A Counterexample to a Question of Hof, Knill and Simon\n\n  * Sébastien Labbé\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/3758\n\nKeywords: Hof, Knill and Simon conjecture, class P, palindromes, stabilizer.\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn this article, we give a negative answer to a question of Hof, Knill and Simon (1995) concerning purely morphic sequences obtained from primitive morphism containing an infinite number of palindromes. Their conjecture states that such palindromic sequences arise from substitutions that are in class $\\mathcal{P}$. The conjecture was proven for the binary alphabet by B. Tan in 2007. We give here a counterexample for a ternary alphabet.\n\n  * PDF\n\nPublished\n\n2014-07-21\n\nHow to Cite\n\nLabbé, S. (2014). A Counterexample to a Question of Hof, Knill and Simon. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 21(3), #P3.11. https://doi.org/10.37236/3758\n\nIssue\n\nVolume 21, Issue 3 (2014)\n\nArticle Number\n\nP3.11--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE EKR PROPERTY FOR FLAG PURE (https://www.maths.usyd.edu.au/u/pubs/publist/preprints/2019/olarte-18.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Every generalized cluster complex C(m, Φ) is pure-EKR. ... Our two main results are evidence for Conjecture 1.2.\nthe number of vertices is of the form mn + 2. The sets of diagonals producing these\n(m+2)-angulations form a pure simplicial complex of dimension n−2, since any (m+2)-\nangulation has n −1 diagonals. More generally, for any ﬁnite root system Φ of rank\nn −1 and a positive integer m, Fomin and Reading [6], generalizing previous work of\nFomin and Zelevinsky [7], introduced the m-th generalized cluster complex of Φ as a\ncertain ﬂag pure complex C(m, Φ) of dimension n −2. They then showed that for root\nsystems of type An−1, the complex C(m, An−1) equals the complex of (m+2)-angulations\nof an (mn+2)-gon. Athanasiadis and Tzanaki [2] proved that the complexes C(m, Φ) are\nvertex-decomposable, hence homotopically equivalent to wedges of (n −2)-spheres, and\ngave a formula for the number of spheres in C(m, Φ).\nWe have computationally veriﬁed that for every n, m with n + m ≤8 and for every\nirreducible root system of rank n −1, the complex C(m, Φ) is pure-EKR, which leads to\nthe following conjectures.\nConjecture 1.5. The complex C(m, An−1) whose facets are the (m + 2)-angulations of\na convex (mn + 2)-gon is pure-EKR.\nConjecture 1.6. Every generalized cluster complex C(m, Φ) is pure-EKR.\nSumming up, we have the following diagram of implications:\nConj. 1.3\nConj. 1.4\nConj. 1.1\nConj. 1.2\nConj. 1.5\nConj. 1.6\nOur two main results are evidence for Conjecture 1.2. On the one hand, we prove the\nconjecture in dimensions up to three.\nTheorem 1.7. Pure, ﬂag simplicial complexes without boundary of dimension at most\nthree are pure-EKR.\nThis has the following immediate consequences.\nCorollary 1.8. Flag pseudo-manifolds, in particular simplicial manifolds, of dimension\nat most three are pure-EKR.\nCorollary 1.9. The generalized cluster complexes C(m, Φ), for arbitrary m, are pure-\nEKR for every Φ of rank at most four. In particular, the complex of (m + 2)-angulations\nof a convex (mn + 2)-gon is pure-EKR for m ≥1 and n ≤5.\nOn the other hand we prove that no intersecting family in which every member touches\na certain ﬁxed edge can have size larger than the largest star:\nTheorem 1.10. Let C be a ﬂag pure simplicial complex without boundary and let ab be\nan edge in C. Let F be an intersecting family of facets of C contained in the union of the\nstars of a and b. Then (at least) one of the two stars has size greater or equal than |F|.\nThe 2-dimensional case of Theorem 1.7 (see Theorem 2.16) is a corollary of the ma-\nchinery developed in Section 2 for proving Theorem 1.10. The 3-dimensional case (see\nTheorem 3.1) is more involved and uses additional machinery presented in Section 3.\nThe paper concludes in Section 4 with various constructions of examples of simplicial\ncomplexes which do not have the pure-EKR property.\n3--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Mathematica® Journal (https://content.wolfram.com/sites/19/2013/12/Miyadera.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.9 years ago; Ryohei Miyadera, Shunsuke Nakamura, Yu Okada, Ryo Hanafusa, and Tomoki Ishikawa ... For a triangular chocolate game satisfying y § Hx +zL ê k, 8x, y, z< is a losing state if ... This conjecture is Proposition 4 when k = 1 and is proved for k = 3 in [6].\n18\nRyohei Miyadera, Shunsuke Nakamura, Yu Okada, Ryo Hanafusa, and Tomoki Ishikawa\ngame coordinates\nfirst player's turn\nx\n7\nz\n7\ny\n14\nk\n1\nnew game\nClick a line to make a move.\nÚ Figure 5. Triangular chocolate games that sati