 all edges colored blue. Such an ordered Ramsey graph is minimal if neither of its proper subgraphs is an ordered Ramsey graph of (H,H'). If H=H' then H itself is called Ramsey finite. We show that a connected ordered graph is Ramsey finite if and only if it is a star with center being the first or the last vertex in the linear order. In general we prove that each Ramsey finite (not necessarily connected) ordered graph H has a pseudoforest as a Ramsey graph and therefore is a star forest with strong restrictions on the positions of the centers of the stars. In the asymmetric case we show that (H,H') is Ramsey finite whenever H is a so-called monotone matching. Among several further results we show that there are Ramsey finite pairs of ordered stars and ordered caterpillars of arbitrary size and diameter. This is in contrast to the unordered setting where for any Ramsey finite pair (H,H') of forests either one of H or H' is a matching or both are star forests (with additional constraints). Several of our results give a relation between Ramsey finiteness and the existence of sparse ordered Ramsey graphs. Motivated by these relations we characterize all pairs of ordered graphs that have a forest as an ordered Ramsey graph and all pairs of connected ordered graphs that have a pseudoforest as a Ramsey graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversité Paris Cité (https://www.imj-prg.fr/theses/pdf/jieao_song.pdf)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; class [P] with primitive line class ℓ, that is, ℓis indivisible in H2(X, Z). ... We focus on the case where the line class ℓis primitive. ... orbit of [P] is unique, to prove the conjecture, it suffices to exhibit a single pair (X, P) ... We reduce the problem to the case of C[n] ,→S[n] using the uniqueness of the\n2.2. LAGRANGIAN PLANE\n35\nConjecture 2.2.10. Let X be a hyperkähler manifold of K3[n]-type containing a\nLagrangian n-plane P. Let ℓbe the class of a line contained in P and let L ∈H2(X, Q) be\nthe dual class. Then the cohomology class of P is given by the degree-n part of exp(L) td1/2\nX\n⌊n\nLn−2k\n2 ⌋\nX\n(2.9)\n[P] =\nh\nexp(L) td1/2\nX\ni\n(n −2k)! td1/2\n2k .\nn =\nk=0\nBakker proved in [Bak17] that for K3[n]-type, there is a unique monodromy orbit for the\nclass [P] with primitive line class ℓ, that is, ℓis indivisible in H2(X, Z). Hence in this case, a\nuniversal formula as above must exist. In lower dimensions, the class [P] has been explicitly\ndetermined: n = 2 by Hassett–Tschinkel [HT09b], n = 3 by Harvey–Hassett–Tschinkel\n[HHT12], and n = 4 by Bakker–Jorza [BJ14]. So the conjecture is verified for n ≤4,\nalthough the above general formula has not been guessed before. Note that for n = 3, 4,\nthe proofs are done by considering linear combination of all possible Hodge classes and\nthen determining the coefficients through hardcore Diophantine analysis. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe first result concerning the class \\(P\\) was given by Tan in [13]. The author extended the class \\(P\\) by morphisms conjugated to the elements of \\(P\\), since it is well-known that fixed points of conjugated morphisms have the same set of factors. This extended class is denoted by \\(P'\\). In [13], it is shown that if a fixed point of a primitive substitution \\(\\varphi\\) over binary alphabet is palindromic, then the substitution \\(\\varphi\\) or \\(\\varphi^2\\) belongs to \\(P'\\). (https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Zuzana-Masakova/publication/273471744_Interval_Exchange_Words_and_the_Question_of_Hof_Knill_and_Simon/links/551419a80cf283ee0834a559/Interval-Exchange-Words-and-the-Question-of-Hof-Knill-and-Simon.pdf)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.6 years ago; The first result concerning the class \\(P\\) was given by Tan in [13]. ... In [13], it is shown that if a fixed point of a primitive substitution \\(\\varphi\\) over binary alphabet is palindromic, then the substitution \\(\\varphi\\) or \\(\\varphi^2\\) belongs to \\(P'\\).\n\nIn [10], Labbé shows that the assumption of binary alphabet in the theorem of Tan is essential. The author exhibited a palindromic fixed point of a morphism \\(\\varphi\\) over a ternary alphabet which is not fixed by any morphism belonging to \\(P'\\). It can be shown that the counterexample is a sequence coding a 3 interval exchange transformation with the permutation (321). ... In this paper, we study sequences that code non-degenerated 3 interval exchange transformation with the same permutation (321).\nThe first result concerning the class \\(P\\) was given by Tan in [13]. The author extended the class \\(P\\) by morphisms conjugated to the elements of \\(P\\), since it is well-known that fixed points of conjugated morphisms have the same set of factors. This extended class is denoted by \\(P'\\). In [13], it is shown that if a fixed point of a primitive substitution \\(\\varphi\\) over binary alphabet is palindromic, then the substitution \\(\\varphi\\) or \\(\\varphi^2\\) belongs to \\(P'\\).\n\nIn [10], Labbé shows that the assumption of binary alphabet in the theorem of Tan is essential. The author exhibited a palindromic fixed point of a morphism \\(\\varphi\\) over a ternary alphabet which is not fixed by any morphism belonging to \\(P'\\). It can be shown that the counterexample is a sequence coding a 3 interval exchange transformation with the permutation (321). However, it is degenerated, i.e., it is a morphic image of a Sturmian word. For details, see Section 7.\n\nIn this paper, we study sequences that code non-degenerated 3 interval exchange transformation with the same permutation (321). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImpartial Triangular Chocolate Bar Games (https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.04954)\nciteturn2academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.0 years ago; Authors: Ryohei Miyadera, Shunsuke Nakamura, Masanori Fukui ... In the triangular chocolate bar game, a position is a $\\mathcal{P}$-position if and only if $x \\oplus y \\oplus z = 0$, where the numbers $x,y,z$ stand for the maximum number of times that the chocolate bar can be cut in each direction.\nTitle: Impartial Triangular Chocolate Bar Games\nAuthors: Ryohei Miyadera, Shunsuke Nakamura, Masanori Fukui\nDate: Tue Nov 14 05:40:55 2017\n\nChocolate bar games are variants of the game of Nim in which the goal is to leave your opponent with the single bitter part of the chocolate bar. The rectangular chocolate bar game is a thinly disguised form of classical multi-heap Nim. In this work, we investigate the mathematical structure of triangular chocolate bar games in which the triangular chocolate bar can be cut in three directions. In the triangular chocolate bar game, a position is a $\\mathcal{P}$-position if and only if $x \\oplus y \\oplus z = 0$, where the numbers $x,y,z$ stand for the maximum number of times that the chocolate bar can be cut in each direction. Moreover, the Grundy number of a position $(x,y,z)$ is not always equal to $x \\oplus y \\oplus z $, and a generic formula for Grundy numbers in not known. Therefore, the mathematical structure of triangular chocolate bar game is different from that of classical Nim.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe EKR property for flag pure simplicial complexes without boundary - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0097316519301864)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.5 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Our motivation is that complexes with these two properties include flag pseudo-manifolds and cluster complexes. ... Chvátal's Conjecture implies Conjecture 1.15((1)) and Borg's relaxation, while it does not immediately imply any pure-EKR properties such as Theorem 1.16 or our Conjecture 1.2. ... Concerning Conjecture 1.3, the answer is that we can neither hope for a strict pure-EKR property nor a 2-intersecting pure-EKR property as can both be seen in the boundary complexes of cross-polytopes:\n\n## Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A\n\nDate: May 2020\n\nArticle: 105205\n\nVolume: Volume 172\n\nView accessibility information\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\n# The EKR property for flag pure simplicial complexes without boundary☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jorge Alberto Olarte ^{a}, Francisco Santos ^{b}, Jonathan Spreer ^{c}, Christian Stump ^{d}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.jcta.2019.105205\n\n  * Article\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (4)\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove that the family of facets of a pure simplicial complex \\mathcal{C} of dimension up to three satisfies the Erdős-Ko-Rado property whenever \\mathcal{C} is flag and has no boundary ridges. We conjecture the same to be true in arbitrary dimension and give evidence for this conjecture. Our motivation is that complexes with these two properties include flag pseudo-manifolds and cluster complexes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYaroslav-the-Wise Novgorod State University (https://www.utupub.fi/server/api/core/bitstreams/787a054d-8908-4a12-b858-f082bae2b151/content)\nciteturn2search19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Theorem 8. Let u be a ﬁxed point of a primitive morphism over a binary ... A counterexample was ... to class P (cf. Example 1).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRyohei Miyadera - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/ryohei-miyadera)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Ryohei Miyadera, Shunsuke Nakamura, Masanori Fukui ... In the triangular chocolate bar game, a position is a $\\mathcal{P}$-position if and only if $x \\oplus y \\oplus z = 0$, where the numbers $x,y,z$ s